@* RequireRole.razor — Phase 0.16
   Reusable component: render children only if the current user is in
   AT LEAST ONE of the supplied roles. If the user lacks all of the
   supplied roles, render nothing (per roadmap Section 12.7: hide,
   don't disable).

   Usage:
     <RequireRole Roles="@(new[] { Roles.Admin, Roles.Manager })">
        <RadzenButton Text="Approve" @onclick="Approve" />
     </RequireRole>

     <RequireRole Roles="@(new[] { Roles.Admin })">
        <RadzenButton Text="Delete user" @onclick="Delete" />
     </RequireRole>

   Why a component instead of just <AuthorizeView Roles="Admin,Manager">?
     - <AuthorizeView> requires a comma-separated string literal, which
       means no compile-time check that "Admin" is actually a valid role.
       Using the Roles static class gives us IntelliSense and refactoring
       safety — rename the role string and every call site updates.
     - <RequireRole> also reads cleaner in pages with many role-gated
       buttons.

   Multiple roles are OR'd (any match shows the content). For AND logic
   (rare: "show only if user is BOTH Admin AND Manager"), use two nested
   <RequireRole> components.

   NOTE on the explicit `@using` directives below: Blazor's _Imports.razor
   is HIERARCHICAL — it only applies to its own folder + subfolders. The
   project-root _Imports.razor SHOULD cover this folder too, but some IDE
   / compiler setups still don't pick it up reliably for sibling folders
   like Authorization/. To eliminate the "Found markup element with
   unexpected name 'AuthorizeView'" warning, the two namespaces
   <AuthorizeView> lives in are imported directly at the top of THIS file
   as a defensive measure. (Even when the root _Imports.razor works,
   having these here is harmless.) *@

@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Authorization

<AuthorizeView Roles="@_rolesCsv">
    @ChildContent
</AuthorizeView>

@code {
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// One or more role names from the <c>Roles</c> static class in
    /// <c>TakOne.Application.Common.Authorization</c>. Pass as an array:
    /// <code>&lt;RequireRole Roles="@(new[] { Roles.Admin, Roles.Manager })"&gt;</code>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string[] Roles { get; set; } = Array.Empty<string>();

    private string _rolesCsv = string.Empty;

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        // AuthorizeView.Roles expects a comma-separated string. We join here
        // rather than at the call site so the API reads naturally.
        _rolesCsv = string.Join(",", Roles ?? Array.Empty<string>());
    }
}